  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This 2023 preprint shows an early but meaningful impact profile: it has 3 citations and 3 documented downstream reuse instances, with uptake already appearing in a journal article, a conference paper, and another preprint. The citing record is still small, but it points to interest in LLM-assisted annotation across computational linguistics and adjacent NLP applications.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Assessing the potential of LLM-assisted annotation for corpus-based pragmatics and discourse analysis: The case of apology has begun to circulate beyond its original preprint form, with evidence of reuse and citation emerging soon after publication. The record shows 3 citations in OpenAlex, all appearing in 2023 and 2024, alongside 3 downstream reuse instances. That pattern suggests an early-stage influence rather than a mature citation footprint, but it is a real sign that the work is being taken up by researchers working on annotation-related questions.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The strongest signal is the nature of the citing landscape. One citing work appears in Humanities and Social Sciences Communications, which indicates visibility in a high-profile interdisciplinary venue. The other citing works are a preprint and a conference paper, showing that the article is being read and used across both formal and rapidly moving research channels. Together, these citations cluster around topics such as topic modeling, natural language processing techniques, explainable AI, and text readability and simplification, which is consistent with the paper\u2019s methodological focus on automating complex linguistic annotation.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The downstream reuse record also matters here. The article is not only cited but reused in multiple work types, including an article, a conference paper, and a preprint. That spread suggests the paper is functioning as a reference point for methodological discussion rather than remaining confined to a single niche. In particular, the presence of reuse in work on text annotation and open science in computational linguistics indicates that the study has entered conversations about how LLMs may support annotation workflows.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">At the same time, the broader attention record is quiet. Event Data shows no tracked online mentions, and the available scite record shows no classified citations. Datacite also reports zero views and downloads for the tracked dataset metadata, while OpenAIRE identifies both the arXiv preprint and a later journal version, suggesting the work exists in both openly accessible and published forms. Overall, the evidence points to a nascent but credible impact story: a specialized methods paper that is beginning to be reused and cited as scholars test the role of LLMs in corpus annotation and discourse analysis.</p>",
